# THE ARG A NILPY THREAD ENTRY ACTUALLY RECEIVES.
#
# __pxxclone's trampoline has exactly one contract -- entry(arg), with arg a raw
# machine word in the first integer argument register -- and it keeps exactly
# one, because Pascal, C, Rust and Zig entries reach the same stub. A NilPy def
# does not fit it: every NilPy parameter is a by-REFERENCE Variant, so the
# callee read that raw word as the ADDRESS of a Variant and dereferenced it.
#
# Measured before the fix, this exact shape: 3 runs of 3 SIGSEGV with nothing
# printed. test_nilpy_thread_clone.npy passes 0 and its worker never mentions
# arg, so the tree's only NilPy thread test could not reach this at all -- the
# defect and the hole had the same shape.
#
# THE VALUE IS 12345 AND NOT 0 OR 1 ON PURPOSE. A thread entry handed a wrong
# word most often gets 0 (the clone stub's own registers are full of zeroes) and
# an assertion expecting 0 could not tell a delivered argument from an undeliv-
# ered one. 12345 is reachable only by actually carrying the caller's word
# through, and the worker adds one so the printed number is not the literal
# either -- a copy of the constant into the wrong place still fails the row.

CLONE_VM = 0x00000100
CLONE_FS = 0x00000200
CLONE_FILES = 0x00000400
CLONE_SIGHAND = 0x00000800
CLONE_THREAD = 0x00010000
CLONE_SYSVSEM = 0x00040000
# CLONE_SYSVSEM IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE, AND NOT BECAUSE LINUX WANTS IT. The
# kernel is happy without it; qemu-user is not -- it implements clone only for
# the complete thread flag set and answers EINVAL for any subset, which is why
# this row failed on aarch64 and arm32 (both emulated) while passing natively
# on x86-64 and i386. The Pascal PAL has carried SYSVSEM all along
# (lib/rtl/palthread.pas, PXX_CLONE_THREAD), which is why Pascal threading
# works on those two targets and this did not.
flags = CLONE_VM | CLONE_FS | CLONE_FILES | CLONE_SIGHAND | CLONE_THREAD | CLONE_SYSVSEM

# ---- PORTABLE CHILD STACK, and why it is a probe rather than a constant ----
#
# THE mmap SYSCALL NUMBER IS PER-TARGET AND NILPY HAS NO TARGET INTROSPECTION:
# x86-64 mmap = 9, i386 and arm32 mmap2 = 192, aarch64 mmap = 222. This file
# used to hardcode 9. That is right on x86-64 and WRONG on the other three,
# where 9 is link/linkat and answers -EFAULT -- so `stk` was -14, the "stack"
# handed to clone was 1048562, and the row still PASSED on i386 because Linux
# accepts any child stack and 1048562 happened to be writable. A test that is
# green because the kernel does not check is not testing the thing.
#
# So ask, in order, and take the first answer that looks like a mapping. Every
# wrong number in the list answers -EFAULT or -EINVAL for THESE arguments on
# the target where it is wrong (a bad pointer in argument 2), so probing costs
# an errno and changes nothing: 9 is link/linkat, 192 is mq_timedreceive on
# aarch64, 222 is timer_create on x86-64 and fadvise64 on the 32-bit pair.
def pxx_stack(size):
    n = 0
    while n < 3:
        if n == 0:
            nr = 9
        elif n == 1:
            nr = 192
        else:
            nr = 222
        r = __pxxrawsyscall(nr, 0, size, 3, 0x22, -1, 0)
        if r > 65535:
            return r
        n = n + 1
    return 0


seen = 0


def worker(arg):
    global seen
    seen = arg + 1


stk = pxx_stack(1048576)
tid = __pxxclone(flags, stk + 1048576, worker, 12345, 0)
print("tid nonzero =", tid > 0)
i = 0
while i < 400000000 and seen == 0:
    i = i + 1
print("child saw =", seen)
